# Platform map

This file is a starting point, written once by `bin/render` and yours to edit
after that. `bin/render` will not overwrite it. What you learn about the estate
goes here; what a template needs goes in `estate.yaml`.

Platform: {{ platform.kind }}, driven with `{{ platform.cli }}`.

| key | app | probe | healthy | notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
{{#services}}| {{key}} | `{{app}}` | `{{url}}` | {{expect}} |{{note_suffix}} |
{{/services}}
**Healthy is not always 200.** A service can answer for itself with a `307` to a
login page or a `404` on a path that was never there. Both mean it is up and
routing. Never probe with `curl -L`: following a login redirect reports green
forever, dead app or not. Probe with no-follow, and treat 2xx/3xx/401/403/404 as
"answering", 5xx or no answer as down. `bin/pulse.sh` already does this every 15
minutes with no model attached, so a red pulse is the signal, not a curl you ran
by hand.

Repo: {{ github.slug }}. WATCH opens issues there; WORK opens pull requests
there and never pushes to main.

Board: {{ github.slug }} issues. States: triage, agent-go, in-progress, pr-open,
merged, deployed, verified, closed, proposal, post-mortem, needs-local.

Deploy path: PR -> merge to main -> CI -> deploy. Logs:
`{{ platform.cli }} logs -a <app> --no-tail`. Status: `{{ platform.cli }} status -a <app>`.

This build: $HERMES_HOME, Hermes Agent pinned to v2026.8.19, model
{{ models.watch }} on WATCH and {{ models.work }} on WORK, hard cost stop at
${{ limits.max_cost_usd_per_task }} per task.

Always run commands through `bin/hermes`. A bare `hermes` on PATH may point at
a different estate.
